From 59ff7853c88b05637834828defacfebfad73fd50 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Samuel Price <thesamprice@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 8 Aug 2026 21:39:47 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 15/16] build: Probe the compiler with the ABI flags when
 building a .rap

rtems-ld finds libraries by asking the target compiler for its
-print-search-dirs.  wscript passed CFLAGS to that probe but not ABI_FLAGS,
which is where the target, the architecture, the ABI and the sysroot live,
so the compiler was probed as if for the build host and answered with the
wrong multilib's directories.

Pass both, and scan both for explicit -L paths rather than only LDFLAGS,
since a build may put them in either.

This does not yet make the explicit -L entries redundant: rtems-ld still
reports "libm.a: Not found" without them even though the directory holding
it now appears in the probe output, so something in how it consumes that
output is still wrong.  That is a separate problem, and the probe being
given the right flags is correct regardless.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 wscript | 17 ++++++++++-------
 1 file changed, 10 insertions(+), 7 deletions(-)

diff --git a/wscript b/wscript
index c7076451b4..d8bf99e52f 100755
--- a/wscript
+++ b/wscript
@@ -553,7 +553,12 @@ class Item(object):
                 "-C",
                 bld.env.CC[0],
                 "-c",
-                " ".join(bld.env.CFLAGS),
+                # The ABI flags carry the target, the architecture and the
+                # sysroot.  Without them the compiler is probed as if for the
+                # build host and reports search directories for the wrong
+                # multilib, so a library such as libm.a is either not found or
+                # is the wrong one.
+                " ".join(bld.env.ABI_FLAGS + bld.env.CFLAGS),
                 "-O",
                 "rap",
                 "-b",
@@ -566,12 +571,10 @@ class Item(object):
             cmd.append(task.outputs[0].abspath())
             cmd.extend([i.abspath() for i in task.inputs[1:]])
             # rtems-ld locates libraries by asking the target compiler for its
-            # "-print-search-dirs".  gcc reports the multilib directories
-            # there, but clang reports only the resource directory and
-            # <sysroot>/lib and ignores -L entirely, so a multilib library
-            # such as libm.a is never found.  Forward the library paths the
-            # build already knows about.
-            for flag in bld.env.LDFLAGS:
+            # "-print-search-dirs", which is answered correctly once the ABI
+            # flags above are passed.  Forward any explicit library paths as
+            # well, from both variables, for builds which set them.
+            for flag in bld.env.ABI_FLAGS + bld.env.LDFLAGS:
                 if flag.startswith("-L"):
                     cmd.extend(["-L", flag[2:]])
             cmd.extend(["-l" + l for l in libs])
